  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 218 PM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA passing shortwave will develop scattered to widespread showers\nand thunderstorms mainly north of the MT Highway 200 corridor \nlate this afternoon through the evening. The incoming trough\nbrings a good synoptic environment for the threat of a few severe\nthunderstorms across North-Central MT, with hail as the primary \nthreat. High PWATs nearing an inch this afternoon will bring an \nisolated flash flood threat to where soils have been saturated \nfrom recent precipitation. Behind this system, a vertically \nstacked low pressure system will move into the area Monday morning\nthrough Tuesday morning, bringing periods of widespread rain for \nmainly North-Central MT. Based on the current low track, periods \nof moderate to heavy rainfall will be possible for the Rocky \nMountain Front and US Highway 2 corridor. This system will also \nbring in cooler fall-like temperatures Monday and Tuesday. Monday \nnight into Tuesday morning, low to mid level winds increases \naround the closed low. This results in breezy to windy conditions \nacross North-Central MT.\n\nAfter the system exits during the day Tuesday, drier conditions\nmove in for Wednesday and most of Thursday. 700mb winds increasing\nalong the Rocky Mountain Front Thursday will also increase \nsurface winds, though confidence is low for high winds. \n\nHeading into late Thursday night into Friday, a cold front will\nre-introduce low end chances for precipitation. Though, better\nchances for precipitation arrived with the next incoming trough\nSaturday and Sunday. Ensembles hint at a cooler system incoming\nnext weekend with snow levels falling to at least the higher\nmountain peaks. Though details and confidence in this next system\nis low. \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe biggest question mark of the day today was cloud cover and\ninstability limiting severe thunderstorm potential today. The \nsynoptics providing both directional and speed shear (40-50kts) \nbring a favorable environment for thunderstorms to become severe \nacross North-Central MT. The morning cloud cover and fog could've \nlimited instability development, but there looks to be a few \nbreaks in the clouds early this afternoon to keep an eye on \nisolated severe storms. Since all the instability will be \nelevated, the storms will be elevated and therefore a primary hail\nthreat, with a low end severe wind gust threat. The best window \nfor severe thunderstorms to develop will be between 6-10pm with \nthe best spatial corridor along and north from the Helena to \nLewistown line. A limiting factor late tonight as storms get \ncloser to the Hi-line is the drop off of instability and increase \nin saturated profiles. Therefore, it seems like those initial \nstorms in the early to mid evening will be the storms to watch.\n\nModels have been consistently showing additional 1-3\" rain\naccumulations along the Rocky Mountain Front and the Sweet Grass\nHills. How far east along US Highway 2 does this extend remains\nuncertain, but this northwest shift in the low track favors the\nRocky Mountain Front for the corridor of heaviest precipitation. \nWith thunderstorms today and tomorrow, some high-res guidance show\nup to isolated 4\" if that area gets good convective rain rates. \nRainfall amounts drop off significantly south of the MT Highway \n200 corridor to less than a quarter of an inch. -Wilson",
